{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPERATIONAL FINDINGS from actually building and running the harness on this host (recorded during execution; some contradict assumptions in the plan text).\n\n1. THE IMAGE DOES NOT CONTAIN JIRA. 'atlas-run' downloads Jira 8.17.1 from maven.atlassian.com AT RUNTIME on first start. Verified in the container log: 'Configured Artifact: com.atlassian.jira.plugins:jira-plugin-test-resources:8.17.1:zip' followed by a stream of 'Downloading from atlassian-public: https://maven.atlassian.com/repository/public/...'. Consequences the harness design must account for:\n   * NETWORK EGRESS to maven.atlassian.com is a hard runtime dependency, not just a one-time image pull. An air-gapped or egress-restricted CI runner cannot start this harness at all.\n   * First start is dominated by that download, so the readiness timeout must cover a large artifact fetch, not just JVM boot.\n   * Mounting a persistent Maven cache (~/.m2) as a volume should make repeat starts dramatically faster; without it every fresh container re-downloads.\n   * SUPPLY-CHAIN/LONGEVITY RISK: if Atlassian ever withdraws the 8.17.1 artifacts from their public Maven repo, the harness stops working even though our Dockerfile digest is pinned. Pinning the base image does NOT pin Jira itself. That is worth stating in the README as the real fragility, and it strengthens the case for the separately-tracked real-instance validation (9895-ac7d-4276-4f08).\n\n2. THE 'CARRIES ITS OWN LICENSE' CLAIM IS UNVERIFIED AND SHOULD NOT BE ASSERTED. What is actually true and observed: no license key had to be supplied to build or start it; the Atlassian SDK's atlas-run provisions the instance itself. Whether that constitutes a bundled license is a legal characterization I have not verified, so the README should describe the observed behavior ('no license key is supplied; the SDK provisions a local developer instance') rather than making a licensing claim.\n\n3. EMULATION IS SEVERE HERE, not a minor tax. Base is linux/amd64; this host is arm64. Upstream README quotes 'first responsive request typically within ~3-5 minutes'. At 10+ minutes on this host the instance was still in the Maven download phase with REST not yet answering. So the plan's readiness timeout must be generous by default AND the README should say a native-amd64 runner is strongly preferred, not merely nicer.\n\nStill measuring whether it comes up at all on emulated arm64; that result decides whether this story is verifiable on THIS host or whether its acceptance evidence must come from an amd64 runner. Recording the finding either way rather than assuming success."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785360856960234001,"uuid":"e39c857d-6bcb-4a3e-a71d-9e5eb6bd8c4d"}